1	## Exercises
2	
3	### Exercise 1: Async Echo Server
4	
5	Build a TCP echo server that handles multiple clients concurrently.
6	
7	**Requirements**:
8	- Listen on `127.0.0.1:8080`
9	- Accept connections and echo back each line
10	- Handle client disconnections gracefully
11	- Print a log when clients connect/disconnect
12	
13	<details>
14	<summary>🔑 Solution</summary>
15	
16	```rust
17	use tokio::io::{AsyncBufReadExt, AsyncWriteExt, BufReader};
18	use tokio::net::TcpListener;
19	
20	#[tokio::main]
21	async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
22	    let listener = TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:8080").await?;
23	    println!("Echo server listening on :8080");
24	
25	    loop {
26	        let (socket, addr) = listener.accept().await?;
27	        println!("[{addr}] Connected");
28	
29	        tokio::spawn(async move {
30	            let (reader, mut writer) = socket.into_split();
31	            let mut reader = BufReader::new(reader);
32	            let mut line = String::new();
33	
34	            loop {
35	                line.clear();
36	                match reader.read_line(&mut line).await {
37	                    Ok(0) => {
38	                        println!("[{addr}] Disconnected");
39	                        break;
40	                    }
41	                    Ok(_) => {
42	                        print!("[{addr}] Echo: {line}");
43	                        if writer.write_all(line.as_bytes()).await.is_err() {
44	                            println!("[{addr}] Write error, disconnecting");
45	                            break;
46	                        }
47	                    }
48	                    Err(e) => {
49	                        eprintln!("[{addr}] Read error: {e}");
50	                        break;
51	                    }
52	                }
53	            }
54	        });
55	    }
56	}
57	```
58	
59	</details>
