195	### Exercise 4: Build a Simple Async Mutex from Scratch
196	
197	Implement an async-aware mutex using channels (without using `tokio::sync::Mutex`).
198	
199	*Hint*: Use a `tokio::sync::Semaphore` with 1 permit to serialize access.
200	
201	<details>
202	<summary>🔑 Solution</summary>
203	
204	```rust
205	use std::cell::UnsafeCell;
206	use std::sync::Arc;
207	use tokio::sync::{OwnedSemaphorePermit, Semaphore};
208	
209	pub struct SimpleAsyncMutex<T> {
210	    data: Arc<UnsafeCell<T>>,
211	    semaphore: Arc<Semaphore>,
212	}
213	
214	// SAFETY: Access to T is serialized by the semaphore (max 1 permit).
215	unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for SimpleAsyncMutex<T> {}
216	unsafe impl<T: Send> Sync for SimpleAsyncMutex<T> {}
217	
218	pub struct SimpleGuard<T> {
219	    data: Arc<UnsafeCell<T>>,
220	    _permit: OwnedSemaphorePermit, // Dropped on guard drop → releases lock
221	}
222	
223	impl<T> SimpleAsyncMutex<T> {
224	    pub fn new(value: T) -> Self {
225	        SimpleAsyncMutex {
226	            data: Arc::new(UnsafeCell::new(value)),
227	            semaphore: Arc::new(Semaphore::new(1)),
228	        }
229	    }
230	
231	    pub async fn lock(&self) -> SimpleGuard<T> {
232	        let permit = self.semaphore.clone().acquire_owned().await.unwrap();
233	        SimpleGuard {
234	            data: self.data.clone(),
235	            _permit: permit,
236	        }
237	    }
238	}
239	
240	impl<T> std::ops::Deref for SimpleGuard<T> {
241	    type Target = T;
242	    fn deref(&self) -> &T {
243	        // SAFETY: We hold the only semaphore permit, so no other
244	        // SimpleGuard exists → exclusive access is guaranteed.
245	        unsafe { &*self.data.get() }
246	    }
247	}
248	
249	impl<T> std::ops::DerefMut for SimpleGuard<T> {
250	    fn deref_mut(&mut self) -> &mut T {
251	        // SAFETY: Same reasoning — single permit guarantees exclusivity.
252	        unsafe { &mut *self.data.get() }
253	    }
254	}
255	
256	// When SimpleGuard is dropped, _permit is dropped,
257	// which releases the semaphore permit — another lock() can proceed.
258	
259	// Usage:
260	// let mutex = SimpleAsyncMutex::new(vec![1, 2, 3]);
261	// {
262	//     let mut guard = mutex.lock().await;
263	//     guard.push(4);
264	// } // permit released here
265	```
266	
267	**Key takeaway**: Async mutexes are typically built on top of semaphores. The semaphore provides the async wait mechanism — when locked, `acquire()` suspends the task until the permit is released. This is exactly how `tokio::sync::Mutex` works internally.
